---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="AC vs. DC Fast Charging: A Simple Guide | SunArth Insights">
	<!-- Article Hero -->
	<header class="article-hero">
		<div class="hero-container">
			<span class="category-badge">Technology</span>
			<h1>AC vs. DC Fast Charging: A Jargon-Free Guide for Businesses</h1>
			<div class="meta-data">
				<span class="author">By Team SunArth</span> •
				<span class="date">July 18, 2026</span> •
				<span class="read-time">6 min read</span>
			</div>
		</div>
		<!-- Hero Image -->
		<div class="hero-image-wrapper">
			<img src="/ac_dc.svg" alt="AC vs DC EV Charging" class="hero-cover" />
		</div>
	</header>

	<main class="article-layout">
		<!-- Sticky Table of Contents -->
		<aside class="sidebar">
			<div class="sticky-toc">
				<h4>Table of Contents</h4>
				<ul>
					<li><a href="#the-difference">The Core Difference</a></li>
					<li><a href="#ac-chargers">When to Choose AC</a></li>
					<li><a href="#dc-chargers">When to Choose DC</a></li>
					<li><a href="#cost-roi">Cost vs. Revenue</a></li>
					<li><a href="#sunarth-edge">The Hardware Advantage</a></li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</aside>

		<!-- Main Article Content -->
		<article class="content">
			<section id="the-difference">
				<p class="lead">
					If you are looking to install EV chargers at your property, you have likely been hit with a wave of confusing acronyms. The biggest hurdle? Deciding between AC and DC chargers.
				</p>
				<p>
					Buying a charger without knowing the difference is like buying a vehicle without knowing if you need a golf cart or a heavy-duty delivery truck. Both are incredibly useful, but putting the wrong one in the wrong place is an expensive mistake. Here is the simple, jargon-free breakdown of how to choose the right hardware for your business.
				</p>

				<h2>1. The Simple Difference: Who Does the Heavy Lifting?</h2>
				<p>
					To keep it extremely simple: Batteries store <strong>DC (Direct Current)</strong> power, but the electricity grid supplies <strong>AC (Alternating Current)</strong> power. Somewhere along the line, that power has to be converted.
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>AC Chargers:</strong> The charger gives AC power to the car, and the car's internal computer does the hard work of converting it to DC. Because the car's converter is small, this process is slower.</li>
					<li><strong>DC Chargers:</strong> The charger itself does the heavy lifting. It converts the power to DC <em>before</em> it even hits the car, pumping it directly into the battery. This makes the charging process incredibly fast.</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section id="ac-chargers">
				<h2>2. AC Chargers: The "Slow and Steady" Destination Choice</h2>
				<p>
					AC chargers (often called Level 2 chargers) are the most common and affordable type of charging hardware. They provide a steady, graceful trickle of power that is very gentle on the vehicle's battery.
				</p>
				<blockquote class="info-card">
					<strong>Best For Long Visits (4+ Hours):</strong> Workplaces, hotels, apartment complexes, and long-term parking garages.
				</blockquote>
				<p>
					If your customers or employees leave their cars parked for half the day or overnight, there is no need to spend money on ultra-fast charging. AC chargers are highly affordable to install, use less grid capacity, and perfectly match a "park and forget" lifestyle.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="dc-chargers">
				<h2>3. DC Fast Chargers: The "Pit Stop" Powerhouse</h2>
				<p>
					DC Fast Chargers are large, industrial-grade machines designed for speed. Depending on the vehicle and the charger's capacity, they can add hundreds of kilometers of range in just 20 to 40 minutes.
				</p>
				<blockquote class="info-card">
					<strong>Best For Quick Stops (15–60 Minutes):</strong> Highway rest stops, fast-food restaurants, coffee shops, and commercial fleet depots.
				</blockquote>
				<p>
					If your business model relies on high customer turnover, DC chargers are your best bet. They act as a powerful magnet for drivers who need a quick top-up while they grab a coffee or a quick meal before hitting the road again.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="cost-roi">
				<h2>4. The Cost Difference vs. Revenue Potential</h2>
				<p>
					The hardware and installation costs between AC and DC are significantly different, which directly impacts how you make your money back.
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>AC Infrastructure:</strong> Low upfront cost. The ROI primarily comes from increased property value, tenant retention (in apartments/offices), and attracting customers to stay longer.</li>
					<li><strong>DC Infrastructure:</strong> Higher upfront investment due to heavy-duty electrical requirements. However, the ROI is direct and rapid. You can charge a premium per-unit rate for the convenience of speed, generating significant direct revenue from the chargers themselves.</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section id="sunarth-edge">
				<h2>5. The SunArth Hardware Advantage</h2>
				<p>
					Whether you choose the steady flow of AC or the high-voltage demands of DC, the hardware must be built to last. A broken charger hurts your reputation and your wallet.
				</p>
				<p>
					At SunArth Industries, our integrated manufacturing process ensures that every charger is engineered for enduring performance. From advanced thermal management that prevents overheating during rapid DC sessions, to industrial-grade enclosures that protect against harsh Indian weather, we build infrastructure that stays online, profitable, and graceful in its operation.
				</p>

				<!-- Redirect CTA to Main Website -->
				<div class="cta-box">
					<p>Need help deciding between AC and DC for your specific property?</p>
					<a href="https://sunarth.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="redirect-link">
						Consult with our Engineering Experts on SunArth.com &rarr;
					</a>
				</div>
			</section>
		</article>
	</main>
</Layout>

<style>
	/* --- Article Hero --- */
	.article-hero { max-width: 1000px; margin: 4rem auto 2rem; padding: 0 2rem; text-align: center; }
	.category-badge { color: var(--primary); font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.9rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 1px; }
	.article-hero h1 { font-size: 3.5rem; margin: 1rem 0; letter-spacing: -1px; }
	.meta-data { color: var(--text-light); font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 3rem; }
	.hero-image-wrapper { width: 100%; border-radius: 16px; overflow: hidden; box-shadow: 0 20px 40px rgba(0,0,0,0.08); }
	.hero-cover { width: 100%; height: 450px; object-fit: cover; display: block; background: #eaeaea; }

	/* --- 2-Column Article Layout --- */
	.article-layout { max-width: 1200px; margin: 4rem auto; padding: 0 2rem; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 250px 1fr; gap: 4rem; }
	.sidebar { position: relative; }
	.sticky-toc { position: sticky; top: 100px; background: var(--white); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border); }
	.sticky-toc h4 { font-size: 1.1rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; padding-bottom: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
	.sticky-toc ul { list-style: none; }
	.sticky-toc li { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; }
	.sticky-toc a { text-decoration: none; color: var(--text-light); font-size: 0.95rem; transition: color 0.2s ease; }
	.sticky-toc a:hover { color: var(--primary); font-weight: 500; }

	/* --- Main Content Typography --- */
	.content { max-width: 750px; font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.8; }
	.content section { margin-bottom: 4rem; }
	.lead { font-size: 1.3rem; color: var(--text-light); margin-bottom: 2rem; }
	.content h2 { font-size: 2.2rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; padding-top: 2rem; }
	.content p { margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
	.content ul, .content ol { margin-bottom: 2rem; padding-left: 1.5rem; }
	.content li { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; }

	/* --- Premium Info Card --- */
	.info-card { background: rgba(14, 122, 77, 0.05); border-left: 4px solid var(--primary); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0; margin: 2.5rem 0; color: var(--text-main); font-weight: 500; }

    /* --- ROI CTA Box --- */
	.cta-box { background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 2rem; margin-top: 2.5rem; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(0,0,0,0.03); }
	.cta-box p { margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 500; color: var(--text-main); font-size: 1.15rem; }
	.redirect-link { display: inline-block; color: var(--primary); font-family: 'Manrope', sans-serif; font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.1rem; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid transparent; transition: all 0.3s ease; }
	.redirect-link:hover { border-bottom-color: var(--primary); transform: translateX(5px); }

	@media (max-width: 1024px) {
		.article-layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
		.sidebar { display: none; }
		.article-hero h1 { font-size: 2.5rem; }
	}
</style>
